 \emph{Implications for practitioners.} Three practical lessons follow from these results. First, a recovery agent should re\hyp{}read the state it will change immediately before writing: neither tested LLM agent did, and they overwrote operator work or acted on a stale dependency. Second, field ownership in the write tool is not enough: it prevented same\hyp{}field overwrites but not writes made after a dependency changed, which needs a precondition check at write time. Third, health checks and the agent's own completion report should not be accepted as proof of safe recovery: every unsafe Kubernetes recovery in both the deterministic and LLM studies passed its health check and produced an unsupported report.
 
-\emph{Construct Validity.} Agents receive explicit target IDs and machine\hyp{}readable preconditions; thus, our tasks measure execution and verification compliance rather than root\hyp{}cause diagnosis or natural\hyp{}language incident parsing. Accepting an approved operator replacement is scored as safe preservation rather than completed recovery. In the Kubernetes study, the workload does not consume the ConfigMap \texttt{mode} at runtime; this isolates contract and precondition violations from application crashes, testing whether passing health checks mask contract violations. Under the initial strict report rule, cited histories did not require post\hyp{}write observations, motivating our stricter post\hyp{}hoc diagnostic and follow\hyp{}up evidence contract.
